Optimal Solution - Use a HashSet to detect the duplicate and identify the missing number in the range 1 to n².
                   “Since numbers are in a fixed range 1 to n², I use a HashSet to detect the duplicate and then scan the range to find the missing number.”

class Solution {
    public int[] findMissingAndRepeatedValues(int[][] grid) { 
        int n = grid.length;            // Grid dimension (n x n)
        int m = grid[0].length;         // Same as n (since square grid)
        Set<Integer> set = new HashSet<>();  // To track seen numbers
        int doubleVal = 0;  // Stores repeated value
        int missing = 0;    // Stores missing value
        // Traverse the grid
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < m; j++) {
                // If number already exists in set → it's the duplicate
                if (set.contains(grid[i][j])) {
                    doubleVal = grid[i][j];
                }
                // Add number to set
                set.add(grid[i][j]);
            }
        }
        // Find missing number in range [1, n*n]
        for (int i = 1; i <= n * n; i++) {
            // If number not found in set → it's missing
            if (!set.contains(i)) {
                missing = i;
                break;
            }
        }
        // Return result as {duplicate, missing}
        return new int[] { doubleVal, missing };
    }
}

Time Complexity :- O(N^2).
Space Complexity :- O(N^2).
